# Resume Matcher Frontend Configuration
# Copy this file to .env.local and update values as needed

# ===========================================
# API Configuration
# ===========================================
# Backend API URL - defaults to "/" (same-origin, proxied by Next.js rewrites).
# Only change this if running the backend on a separate host/port during development.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=/

# Request timeout in milliseconds for long-running calls (resume tailoring),
# applied to both the Next.js API proxy and the client fetch. Default 600000
# (600s). Raise it for slow local LLMs; bounded to [30000, 1800000].
# IMPORTANT: keep it in sync with the BACKEND's REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
# (= this / 1000) — the shortest of the two aborts first.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=600000

# ===========================================
# Authentication (Supabase, Google OAuth only)
# ===========================================
# Google is the only sign-in method; there is no email/password flow. Supabase
# owns identity, and the frontend forwards its access token to the backend,
# which verifies it and partitions all data by the user id inside it. Resume
# files never go to Supabase — they stay in the backend's own database.
#
# Leave BOTH blank for single-user local mode: no sign-in screen, all data in
# one implicit local account. Setting them switches the app to multi-user mode,
# and the backend's SUPABASE_URL must point at the SAME project
# (see apps/backend/.env.example) or the two will disagree.
#
# One-time setup in your Supabase project:
#   1. Authentication -> Sign In / Providers -> enable Google, and paste the
#      client ID + secret from a Google Cloud OAuth 2.0 "Web application".
#   2. In Google Cloud, add this Authorized redirect URI:
#        https://<your-project>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback
#   3. Supabase -> Authentication -> URL Configuration:
#        Site URL:      http://localhost:3000     (your real origin in prod)
#        Redirect URLs: http://localhost:3000/auth/callback
#                       https://your-domain/auth/callback
#
# Both values are publishable by design — the anon key grants only what your
# Supabase policies grant, and Next.js inlines them into the client bundle.

# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
#
# NAMING: Supabase renamed this key. New projects show a "publishable" key
# (sb_publishable_...) under Project Settings -> API Keys; older projects show
# an "anon" JWT, sometimes now under a "Legacy API keys" tab. They are the same
# kind of credential and BOTH WORK. You may use either variable name:
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJ...
# Whichever is set wins (publishable is checked first).
#
# Do NOT use the "secret" key (sb_secret_...) or "service_role" — those bypass
# your security rules and would be inlined into the browser bundle.
#
# The URL must be the project ROOT (https://<ref>.supabase.co) with NO path.
# The Data API page shows ".../rest/v1/" — that is the REST endpoint, not this.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-publishable-or-anon-key

# ===========================================
# Development Server (optional)
# ===========================================
# To change the frontend port, use: npm run dev -- -p 3001
# Or add to package.json scripts: "dev": "next dev --turbopack -p 3001"
#
# If you change the frontend port, make sure to update:
# 1. Backend CORS_ORIGINS to include your new frontend URL
# 2. Backend FRONTEND_BASE_URL to point to your new frontend URL
